WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.400
Film Courage: From being at Showtime what
did you learn about a flat script versus a

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.400
viable script?

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.400
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:14.050
Media Arts: To me I’m looking for something
that’s pretty basic, does it grab me and

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:23.660
does it keep me (however they accomplish that).

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.070
Does it flow, does it have a natural flow?

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.220
Does it make me want to turn the pages?

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.940
In my training I learned somewhat how to do
that and then in subsequent experiences and

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.920
study and we talk about it in the books [The
Science of Screenwriting: The Neuroscience

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.110
Behind Storytelling Strategies and Screenwriting:
The Sequence Approach] the techniques that

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.110
you can actually use.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:47.100
There are tools you can use to keep the audience
engaged, but essentially that’s it.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.630
I wasn’t looking (nor was I instructed to
look for) any particular type of material.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.250
I was just handed things.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.489
And some of the things I did were reading
stage plays.

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:04.509
Like can we make this into a showtime on Broadway
production.

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:12.470
Sometime they’d send me off to see a theater
group and I’d have to give a report on my

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.250
judgment on that.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:23.130
But beyond something that grabs you and keeps
your attention that’s pretty much it.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:28.349
Now there was one script and it will have
to remain nameless.

00:01:28.349 --> 00:01:34.539
There was one script that I remember for a
couple years that I was doing that and I thought

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:35.969
this is funny.

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:37.259
This is smart.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:38.709
This is clever.

00:01:38.709 --> 00:01:40.700
I sure hope this gets made somehow.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:47.100
And that was 1983-1984.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.310
And time went on and sure enough it got made
20 years later.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.649
And I thought that’s great.

00:01:54.649 --> 00:02:01.689
Same title but by that time it had been so
rewritten that it was a famous disaster and

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:02.700
that was just too bad.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.549
But that would be all example.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:10.179
There was one, the cleverness of the writing,
the way the characters were so sharp, the

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.540
way it flowed, made me remember.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.319
It stood out in a pile of material.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:19.360
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:24.330
Why do you think so many…whether it’s
hands got into the pie or whatever?

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:29.170
Why do you think it had such a fresh voice
and then it was almost…these are just my

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.690
words by destroyed?

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.760
Maybe that’s too strong?

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.379
Professor Gulino: Well that’s not too strong.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:45.540
The mode of production at least for big movies
is a problem.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:51.421
Because when you have a big movie and this
wound up being a big studio movie just so

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:57.989
much pressure that it work that there are
defensive things that happen that we need

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:02.239
to make sure that “Okay, I’m not sure
this draft is right?

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:04.040
Let’s get another writer in.”

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:12.799
There is so much at stake that it has an effect
on the product and it can become incoherent

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:14.519
and it is a collaborative medium.

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:20.120
And there is always going to be (even in the
best of circumstances) you can have a great

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:26.590
writer, director, producer, cast and script
and it can still fall flat (it’s imprecise).

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:35.139
But storytelling tends to flatten out in this
situations because one of the elements that

00:03:35.139 --> 00:03:39.249
I talk about more in the first book [Screenwriting:
The Sequence Approach] is dramatic irony.

00:03:39.249 --> 00:03:46.459
Dramatic irony is of course Alvin Hidrock
used it to create suspense to create a story

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:51.969
that involves withholding some information
from the audience, withholding some information

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:55.450
from the characters when you reveal it.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:04:01.120
That requires someone who is really watching
it and imagining it in their own mind at the

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.120
time.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.609
And if you’ve got a lot of people involved
somebody is going to go “Oh my goodness!

00:04:06.609 --> 00:04:08.390
Are we sure the audience is going to get this?

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.079
Well we better make it clearer.”

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:14.560
And if it’s clear, it risks being boring.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:23.550
The three questions that Frank Daniel shared
with us that I still think apply to any story.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:29.380
He said there are three questions that you
ask when you are crafting a story.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:32.949
One - what does the main character want and
what are they trying to avoid?

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:34.540
That’s question one.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.699
Two - What does the main character know and
what does the main character not know?

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:41.620
And then the third question - what does the
audience know?

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:42.990
What does the audience not know?

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:49.720
And I don’t think most writers realize that
you have control over those second two.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.900
Especially when I work with students, that
the audience doesn’t have to know everything

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.180
all the time.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.980
And the characters don’t have to know everything
all the time.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.460
You can withhold some information, you can
play games.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:07.040
In fact my takeaway from the book that [Chapman
Professor Connie Shears] and I did last year

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:15.960
The Science of Screenwriting was applying
constructivist psychology to the storytelling

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.289
process.

00:05:17.289 --> 00:05:24.220
This was something that was in the eighties
film critic David Bordwell came out with a

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:33.870
blockbuster scholarly work in which he squarely
placed storytelling in the realm of constructivist

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:34.870
psychology.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:41.419
And that simply means that most of our experience
in life, most of our experience in reality

00:05:41.419 --> 00:05:47.510
is based not on knowledge but on inferences
we make based on clues.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:53.860
So that you have what is called top down processing.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:58.780
Bottom up processing is you see things and
it goes into your brain and you make note

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.830
of it and you store information.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:07.139
Top down is you see something in the world
and your brain automatically compares what

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:10.580
you’ve experienced before with what you
are seeing and then you make a conclusion.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.199
There are short cuts that we’re doing all
of the time.

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:17.580
You’ve never seen the back of this chair
but you’ve seen chairs so you assume the

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.090
back looks like any other chair.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:25.280
It would be a surprise twist if the back of
this chair had a dragon hiding out there because

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:31.080
that’s not normal (what you’re normally
associating with that concept).

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:43.099
So when you’re telling a story, if you understand
that is how audiences are responding, how

00:06:43.099 --> 00:06:48.659
they experience a movie is they are looking
for clues and they are going to put them together

00:06:48.659 --> 00:06:52.139
and they are actively involved in constructing
a reality.

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:57.970
You are the one as a screenwriter you are
giving them the clues, you are turning them

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:03.110
(and again in Frank Daniel’s terms), you’re
making them the smartest person in the room.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:07.000
You are making them brilliant because they
are seeing all of these clues.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.030
You are the one actually giving it to them.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:14.250
But they think they are figuring it out and
they are going to try to anticipate where

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:18.020
you are going because they have seen movies
before.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:21.550
This is a conceptual framework called a schema.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.030
They’ve seen a movie.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.480
They know how movies are.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.560
They know they tend to have a character that
does this or that.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.669
And when you know that you can play games
with them.

00:07:30.669 --> 00:07:37.689
And that’s for me the most fun thing about
screenwriting is creating worlds and driving

00:07:37.689 --> 00:07:39.539
people crazy and getting in their heads.

00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:42.810
And you can learn how to get into their heads.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:49.190
Just a very simple example suppose I show
you a movie and there’s a husband and he’s

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:59.930
buying his wife flowers and chocolate and
an anniversary card on his way home from work,

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:00.930
okay?

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:06.710
Then meanwhile you see the wife has got a
and she’s hiding it in the bedroom drawer,

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:07.710
okay?

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.940
What are you going to think?

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.460
Well it’s pretty obvious isn’t it?

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.699
He wants to make love and she has other plans.

00:08:16.699 --> 00:08:24.250
That’s where the audience is going to go
and you could pay it off where he comes home

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.550
with the flowers and she pulls out and him.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:32.850
Or you could then disclose later that he’s
a collector and the surprise is this is a

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:35.610
present for him, this is something he’s
been looking for.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:40.450
She saved up for it and she wants to give
it to him as an anniversary present.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:44.450
And then you find out that he poisoned the
chocolate.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:50.390
So you just told a twist which is telling
a twist that is telling two stories at the

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.390
same time.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.750
The one the audience thinks its seeing and
the one it is actually seeing.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:59.570
And you are relying when you do that kind
of thing the audiences propensity to figure

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:05.260
it out and be smarter than you and once you’ve
got them going that way you can have all kinds

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:06.300
of fun.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:12.590
That’s one element of the constructivist
psychology that we mentioned in the book and

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:17.510
have examples of the filmmakers doing that
kind of thing.

